<SiteLayout title="How to Shift Subtitle Timing by Milliseconds | ConvertAnyFile" description="Learn how to fix subtitles that appear too early or too late by applying an exact positive or negative timing offset. Includes examples, safety limits, and local browser steps.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>Shift subtitle timing</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Subtitle guide</span><h1>How to shift subtitle timing when captions are early or late</h1><p>If every cue is consistently ahead of or behind the audio by the same amount, an exact timing offset can correct the whole subtitle track without manually editing each cue.</p></div></section>
<ArticleFrame toc={toc} related={related} relatedTitle="Related subtitle tools & guides">
<div class="quick-answer" id="quick-answer"><strong>Quick answer</strong><p>Measure how far the subtitles are early or late, convert that delay to milliseconds, and enter it in the <a href="/subtitle-converter/">Subtitle Converter</a>. Use a positive value to move cues later and a negative value to move cues earlier.</p></div>
<h2 id="when">When a global timing shift is the right fix</h2><p>A timing offset works when the subtitle track has the correct pacing but the entire track is displaced by a consistent amount. For example, if dialogue appears about 1.35 seconds before the speaker starts across the whole video, shifting every cue by +1350 ms can solve the problem.</p><p>It does not fix subtitles that gradually drift out of sync. Progressive drift usually points to a frame-rate mismatch, a different video cut, or timing that needs rescaling rather than a constant offset.</p>
<h2 id="calculate">How to calculate the offset</h2><p>First pick a clear spoken line and compare the subtitle appearance time with the actual audio. If the subtitle appears 0.78 seconds too late, the correction is approximately -780 ms. If it appears 2.4 seconds too early, the correction is approximately +2400 ms.</p><ul><li><strong>Subtitles too early:</strong> use a positive offset.</li><li><strong>Subtitles too late:</strong> use a negative offset.</li><li><strong>1 second:</strong> 1000 milliseconds.</li></ul>
<h2 id="steps">How to shift subtitle timing</h2><ol><li>Open the <a href="/subtitle-converter/">Subtitle Converter</a>.</li><li>Add the subtitle file and wait for full-file preflight.</li><li>Choose the desired output format.</li><li>Enter the exact offset in the Timing shift field, such as <code>1350</code> or <code>-780</code>.</li><li>Convert and download the corrected copy.</li><li>Test several points near the beginning, middle, and end of the video.</li></ol>
<h2 id="limits">Why timing tools need safety limits</h2><p>A negative shift can push the first cue before 00:00. Instead of silently clipping invalid timestamps, ConvertAnyFile fails the transform when a cue would become negative. The converter also limits timing offsets to a defined range so an accidental extra zero does not rewrite an entire batch into obviously unusable timing.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Check for drift</strong><p>If the correction looks right at the beginning but wrong later, do not keep adding larger offsets. The problem is probably not a constant delay.</p></div>
<h2 id="faq">Common subtitle timing questions</h2><h3>What does +1000 ms do?</h3><p>It moves every cue one second later.</p><h3>What does -1000 ms do?</h3><p>It moves every cue one second earlier, as long as no cue would become negative.</p><h3>Can I shift SRT and VTT?</h3><p>Yes. The Subtitle Converter applies supported timing transforms through the same timed-text engine.</p><h3>Can this fix subtitles for a different video cut?</h3><p>Only if the difference is a constant offset. Added or removed scenes usually require more complex retiming.</p>
